-- title:
--   An open cube is contained in the closed cube of the same side
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$, and let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Writing $A_h(u)$ for the open cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ and $\overline{A}_h(u)$ for the closed cube with the same centre and side, one has
--   $$A_h(u) \;\subseteq\; \overline{A}_h(u).$$
--   In coordinates, the strict inequalities $|x_i - u_i| < h/2$ for all $i$ imply the non-strict ones $|x_i - u_i| \le h/2$.
--
--   The lemma is the elementary passage from the open tiles, which are convenient because their measure and their mutual intersections are easy to control, to the closed ones, which are convenient because they are compact and because a closed cube contains its boundary. Having both versions available with an inclusion between them lets an argument use whichever is convenient at each step.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis is imposed on $h$; for $h \le 0$ the open cube is empty and the inclusion is vacuous.
